Peter it's lovely to see here at  collector mania today what are you  surprised that people don't ask you  about your time has been on the show the  one thing that does surprise me is they  never ask about the technical abilities  of the time because people make jokes  about you know the Robley sets and all  the rest of it not very wide angle shots  and people running up down the same  corridor slightly different camera  angles and so on but I mean the  technical ability of the time was you  know doctor who was at the cutting edge  of it really and it was money more than  anything else that stopped it being  clever I thought to be honest the doctor  who would have died when Star Trek  started because suddenly there was a  show which was a long similar lines at  least it was sci-fi and everything that  had a huge amount of money pumped into  it and doctor who was being made for  threatens and somehow it survived and I  reckon it was the quality of the writing  and possibly one could even say was it  was the quality of Bill Hartnell's  acting you know he created something  which really has this on Garrety now  when you look back and you think gosh I  was with the first incarnation of the  doctor what does it meet you feel I'm  chuffed immense I mean it's great and  for me he is the doctor he always it'll  never be anyone else who can usurp him  from that he created it and so every  doctor that's come after doesn't matter  how good they are as actors and they've  been some cracking actors in there  they're not the doctor not for me but no  it's yeah it's I'm very proud of the  fact that I was in it it's quite  interesting in I did 46 episodes in  total counting the one when I wasn't  playing Stephen miles play Morton dill  that was in a year and Colin Baker was  the doctor for three years he did 29  episodes I mean the difference is that  when we did it was very much like weekly  rep you know we did a different show  each week you went in started rehearsing  on that on the Monday you did the show  recorded on the Friday as if it was a  live thing and it was you know it was a  bit of a sausage factory  what did you enjoy most about playing  see even though I J of characters I was  allowed to play we did the historical  stories I thought were great  unfortunately they don't really exist  the massacre doesn't exist the  mythmakers doesn't exist the savages  doesn't really exist though that was  more sci-fi than historical so it's  disappointing in that respect the time  medal is the only one that has a  historical edge that does survive but  that was a real mix of time travel plus  the the history of the the Saxon times  in Britain so see they were always the  interesting ones for me I didn't like  working with the Daleks and the  Chumley's and all that I didn't find  them interesting and they're certainly  not scary when you're working with them  in the scene do you know were you scared  of the Daleks no but I never say that to  kids always oh yeah of course there's  going to be a show about the making or  the beginnings of doctor who isn't on  the BBC very shortly a venture in space  and time cancer is a non-speaking part  in that but I I'm not involved in it but  I like to think I helped towards the  finished script because I had a nice  evening with Mark Gatiss and a few  anecdotal things I'll be interested to  see if they're in the in the film I met  I've never met David Bradley before  today I think he's brilliant casting for  absolutely brilliant casting and I know  that Anika wills has seen it and she  says you actually watch it mrs. bill  Hartnell's fantastic performance he's  not trying to do an impression he's or  an impersonation I should say it's an  impression it's fantastic what did I  like about the man behind the doctor III  well I admired bill very much as an  actor before I joined I mean he made 70  films and one forgets like you know  prolific film actor and it was great to  walk into the rehearsal room on the  first episode that I did to see Bill  Hartnell standing there I was very  impressed I really was impressed the  nice thing about doing the show was it  was at the time when better actors were  being engaged  to play the other parts and it could  that grew because prior to that to a  certain extent actors tend to frown a  little bit at television because there  wasn't a lot of television worked for  actors mostly they would carry on  working on the stage so the stage was  the thing so to work on television was  young people put down their noses a bit  at it so as great actors started to come  into the serial that was wonderful as  well I mean I felt quite humble at times  I was a regular in the serial but you've  got people like Andre Morrell coming in  max Adrian great actors we've got our  next doctor already lined up everywhere  he's going to be Peter Capaldi what do  you think Peter will bring to the role  of the doctor I think he's a great  brother casting funnily enough you know  he's older now I think the bill Hartnell  was when he played the doctor originally  don't think he looks at mine I think he  can bring a lot to it I don't think  you'll swear much I hope not if I'm  certainly way but I like his acting I  like his style I'm sure he'll be very  good but who knows what the scripts are  like I mean that the scripturally  determine how it's going to be and what  Peter brings to it will be between him  and the producers and what he's allowed  to get away with and I mean who knows I  don't know how much actors are allowed  to dictate what happens so you know the  production will be what it is his  performance will be what it is I'm  looking forward to it because I think  it'll be very good and if you were asked  to return to the TARDIS at any point or  return to the universe as any character  would you like to do that and who would  you like to return I would leap at it I  wouldn't care as I would love a part in  it I would be delighted to play don't  even want a leading part just give me a  partner I'd be very very happy we'd love  to see you in it because  thanks so much for talking to us today  you're welcome  you
 

